WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.840
welcome to the show, which almost didn't happen
today.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:06.149
We were probably 10 minutes from having to
cancel today's show.

00:00:06.149 --> 00:00:07.149
It is quite

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:09.750
a story and I'll tell it on the bonus show
today.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.219
Uh, it's a good one.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:15.059
There are two concepts that I've been talking
about which really are necessary concepts

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:19.050
to understand if we want to interpret it and
understand the Trump presidency.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.760
And these two concepts are the Overton window
and creeping normality.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.030
And these are two terms that will help to
shed light onto how we are all truly becoming

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.640
numb to different degrees, to Donald Trump's
lawlessness.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.190
And there's a really good letter to the editor
in the La Times from over the weekend, which

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:46.140
said that much like a victim of abuse who
gets hit once and then twice and then 10 times

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.170
you start to become numb to that abuse.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:55.289
And in the same way we all to some degree
have become numb to Donald Trump's lawlessness,

00:00:55.289 --> 00:01:01.250
his ignoring of rules and regulations, his
being a scofflaw and most importantly his

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.250
suffering.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.960
No consequences from it either.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.980
And the letter to the editor in the La Times
very astutely points out many of the examples

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:14.660
of this, the abuses of power and the corruption
and the cronyism and the violations of the

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.090
Hatch Act.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.570
But I understand what it is that's happening
here.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:24.900
The outrage and the disarray that people are
experiencing as emotions over the Trump presidency

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:32.320
are logical emotions, but they are emotions
that consume a lot of human energy, of emotional

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.320
energy.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.380
And at a certain point, most reasonable people
will sort of give in and move on because it's

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.160
just draining and exhausting to continually
experience those emotions.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:47.340
And when you add to this a significantly radical
move of the Overton window to the right and

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.680
the creeping normality that we have experienced
over the last two and a half years, it's a

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.840
recipe for an authoritarian president who's
very actions have made the population numb

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.160
to his blatant disregard for what's right
and moral and sensible.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.690
As a reminder, the Overton window is sort
of a range of what's acceptable for debate

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.259
in the public discourse.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:12.760
Uh, what are the, if we talk about abortion
or we talking about taxation, what are the

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:19.110
sort of accepted boundaries on the left and
right of that debate and the right has significantly

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.860
benefited from pulling that over to the window
to the right, which moves the middle to the

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.680
right as well and creates more of an uphill
battle for the left to fight against.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.680
Creeping.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.700
Normality is another term where you can't
just jump to many of the debates we're having

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.689
today, they have to creep in as normal debates.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:42.469
If Trump on day one ordered a strike on Iran
and then canceled it at the last second and

00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:47.499
we found out that it was because he was told
that that last hack and how many people would

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:53.260
die, there would be much more outraged than
there was just two weeks ago when that very

00:02:53.260 --> 00:03:00.579
thing happened because this new status quo
under Trump first had to become the baseline

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:01.590
status quo.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:07.239
And it's really important to understand that
normalization doesn't mean people start to

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:10.200
think the things Trump is doing are okay.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.870
What's Trump doing is fine, is not what creeping
normalization means.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.900
It means that you sort of start white flagging
it and throwing your arms up in the air and

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.620
saying, this is all insane.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.989
I've got no power over any of this stuff.

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:27.170
I'm going to go play a video game or watch
a Netflix show, which is in a sense, a sort

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:32.040
of logically adaptive behavior for the individual
health of individuals.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.870
Although it's extremely unhealthy for our
society to allow this creeping normalization

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.689
of radical ideas to seep in.

00:03:38.689 --> 00:03:44.019
Now, I want you, not to get me wrong, there
are many Trumpists who do genuinely think

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:46.180
everything that Trump is doing is okay.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.510
I'm not talking to them in this segment, okay?

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.939
I'm talking to people with a good head on
their shoulders.

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.299
People who don't think that what's going on
is okay, but are experiencing that fatigue,

00:03:57.299 --> 00:04:02.079
uh, that I've described before as well, which
is why I'm trying to take weekends totally

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:06.370
off from news and politics as I did over the
long a 4th of July break.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.189
It's not that Trump, uh, as an example.

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:15.249
It's not that when, when Trump glorifies dictators
and criticized as our allies, we think it's

00:04:15.249 --> 00:04:16.319
fine.

00:04:16.319 --> 00:04:18.000
It's that we've become used to it.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.080
And what can we really do about it anyway,
other than of course, vote him out in 2020,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.620
which I hope we're all going to do.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:24.860
So this is really dangerous.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.400
It's going to take a really long time to repair
the damage that has been done.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.190
And the removal of Trump from office in 2020
is merely step one of, I don't know how many

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.360
of many, many steps.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.639
And the goal here is that we have to understand
that we have to some degree become numb and

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:47.870
we have to fight what is admittedly that natural
reaction if we can, because we don't want

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:54.400
that numbness to allow any level of indifference
of inaction when we can have an effect on

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.840
the society around us, on the country, around
us.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:04.340
Uh, and this also relates to the very disturbing
recent trend of extreme right wing ideology

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.210
becoming mainstream, at least in some circles.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:06:35.250
And that's exactly what I want to talk about
next.

